<% content_for :title, "Sign in · Tastatur" %>

<%= render layout: "devise/shared/auth_card",
           locals: { title: "Sign in", subtitle: "Welcome back." } do %>
  <%# `turbo: false` SO A PASSWORD MANAGER CAN OFFER TO SAVE THE PASSWORD, which
      is the same reason every Stripe button carries it (§12) and the same
      mechanism: Turbo submits with `fetch` and applies the redirect with
      pushState, so the browser never performs a navigation. A manager decides to
      prompt by watching for a form with a password field being submitted and a
      document load following it. Measured on the wire before this was added, the
      POST went out as `resourceType=Fetch` and `Page.frameNavigated` never fired
      at all — sign-in worked perfectly and nothing was ever offered to be saved.
      With it, the same POST is a `Document` and the navigation fires.

      Safe for the analytics here, and that is not luck: "Sign In Submitted" is
      annotated on the BUTTON, so it is counted on the click (see the note below
      and app/javascript/controllers/analytics_controller.js). The events
      annotated on a FORM are counted from `turbo:submit-end` and would have been
      silently deleted by this attribute — which is why the other three
      credential forms moved theirs to the server rather than just switching
      Turbo off. See SelfMeasurement. -%>
  <%= form_for(resource, as: resource_name, url: session_path(resource_name),
               html: { class: "card card-body space-y-5", data: { turbo: false } }) do |f| %>
    <%= f.field :email, "Email", as: :email_field, autofocus: true, autocomplete: "username" %>
    <%= f.field :password, "Password", as: :password_field, autocomplete: "current-password" %>

    <% if devise_mapping.rememberable? %>
      <%= f.check_box_field :remember_me, "Stay signed in on this device" %>
    <% end %>

    <%# Annotated on the button rather than the form, so a rejected password is
        counted too: Devise recalls this screen with a 422 and the analytics
        controller ignores submissions the server refused. Whether the password
        was right is not this page's to say — "Signed In" is recorded by the
        server, which is also the only place that knows whether it was this
        account's first. See SelfMeasurement. %>
    <div class="pt-1">
      <%= f.submit "Sign in", class: "btn btn-primary w-full justify-center",
                   data: { analytics_event: "Sign In Submitted" } %>
    </div>
  <% end %>
<% end %>
